A||||14||FieroFanaticus2||Anybody know anything about the PORSCHE 924??  pic inside||||1||
Z||000000||FieroFanaticus2||12-17-2001||09:02 AM||||Ok, so a friend of mine has a Porsche 924 that has been sitting for the last 4 years.  It needs a new alternator and a tune up.  Since it's been sitting outside in wisconsin it somehow had water leak into it and some of the leather interior now has mildew on it.<BR>His Father died 3 years ago and left him some $1.8 million, 3 houses and a bunch of other stuff.  So money doesn't mean much to him.  He's offered to sell me the Porsche for $200.  I have never personally seen the car, but do know that before it sat here in wisconsin my friend lived in Florida, where the car was bought and driven.  This is the only picture I have of it.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Joe_Porsche.jpg"> <P>||||reg||1
Z||000001||yo_sweet||12-17-2001||09:09 AM||||those ones are cheap always were , they will never be worth much money..but for $200 you can't go wrong<P>------------------<BR>1986 Fiero SE 2.8lv6<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/dance.gif">  <P><BR>www.geocities.com/acme_s2000/intro.html<BR>yo_sweet!||||reg||1
Z||000002||Oreif||12-17-2001||09:12 AM||||924's were the lower version of the 944. Less expensive but not bad cars. They were not "fast" but not bad. $200 sounds like s good deal. In good condition they run up to about $4000.00 So if you can get the car fixed up for a less than $2000, You could sell it for more or use it as a daily driver and keep the miles off the Fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Raydar||12-17-2001||09:44 AM||||Go here...<BR> <A HREF="http://www.renegadehybrids.com/main.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.renegadehybrids.com/main.htm</A> <P>They manufacture a kit to install a Chevy V-8 in a 944. Supposed to be a bolt-in. I'm guessing the 924 would be the same.<P>The 924 was the first "economy" front-engine Porsche. It wasn't really fast, even new.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000004||rogergarrison||12-17-2001||09:51 AM||||924 is only 1 from the bottom, up from 914. I sold 1 that ran good but needed some elec work on stuff like windows, headlites. had new paint job. Sold it for $1200. Why isnt he just giving it to you for free? $200 isnt a drop out of 1.8 million.  Hell, if I had 2 million, Id give all my closest friends brand new cars.<p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 12-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||Formula88||12-17-2001||10:32 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Raydar:<BR><B>Go here...<BR> <A HREF="http://www.renegadehybrids.com/main.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.renegadehybrids.com/main.htm</A> <P>They manufacture a kit to install a Chevy V-8 in a 944. Supposed to be a bolt-in. I'm guessing the 924 would be the same.<P>The 924 was the first "economy" front-engine Porsche. It wasn't really fast, even new.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Has anyone checked into their Kit Car transaxles?  They offer mid engine setup transaxles that bolt to Small Block Chevy engines - up to a Porsche 993 6-speed, not to mention the 930 transaxle that can hold up to 700 horsepower according to their website.  Might be something for the V8 Fiero guys to check into.||||reg||1
Z||000006||TRiAD||12-17-2001||11:29 AM||||924s were basically Porsche bottom-feeders, I disagree with Roger here, the 914 was a better car, at least for competition. <P>Now, parts, restorations, maintenance are all expensive as heck on Porsches in general, and the 924 is no different, even though it (and the 944) were actually manufactured by Audi for Porsche. <P>But, like he said earlier...you can't go wrong for $200...I'd buy it and try to throw an SBC or something in it!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://communities.msn.com/_Secure/0NgAAAMsSN*Wryq60N*CeWB!hXjfeIaMEc1YxPH9aPzaIXm!17ZpdBFE0Yk*d*dHNFG*GR3SFyLa8KJgh1IKe2Q/xmas-sig.jpg">       <BR><B><I><FONT COLOR="red">Merry Christmas!</FONT><BR><FONT COLOR="blue">Project MR1 ~ triadtuning@hotmail.com</B><BR>Photoshop rendition of planned modifications<BR>AIM ~ Michael Blue 72</I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000007||Raydar||12-17-2001||12:04 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by TRiAD:<BR><B>924s were basically Porsche bottom-feeders...it (and the 944) were actually manufactured by Audi for Porsche. <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>[rumor] I've heard that the 924 was actually going to be released as a Volkswagen. [/rumor]<P><P>------------------<BR>Raydar<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>From the Department of Redundancy Department.</FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000008||MinnGreenGT||12-17-2001||12:28 PM||||My brother bought & restored a '79 924 when he was 16.  Sold it to a loser who didn't pay for it and beat it- so we ended up reposessing the car. It really only needs the transaxle replaced (we have it, just needs to be done).  But my brother's wife told him that as soon as it was fixed- he had to sell it (he's got an '88GT and just bought a '98 Ducati 748). So it's been sitting for the last 4 or 5 years- it's stupid!<P>It really is a pretty nice car, but parts are really expensive. It was cheaper to find a replacement transaxle ($400) than to order the bad gears for the original one ($950+).  Still, $200 you can't go wrong.  Turn around and sell it for parts if you have to.  And cleaning a stinky interior isn't that bad- just some good cleaner and a sunny day or 3 to let it air out!||||reg||1
Z||000009||SwissFormula||12-17-2001||12:35 PM||||The 924 has a Audi engine, so replacement parts can be cheaper than the ones for 944 models. My sister had a 944 (she totaled it this year in Cannes/France   <IMG SRC="http://www.stopstart.fsnet.co.uk/smilie/frown_2.gif"> ) she nedded a new alternator about 3 weeks before the accident, was an expensive repair: SFr. 1200.- that's ~700US$ she received the bill AFTER the car was gone! Kinda hard...<BR>The 924 has something like 140HP, so its not a very fast car. Reliability, good handling and brakes are pro's.<BR>If you can fix it for little $$$ - go for it!<P>------------------<BR>Roy<P>88 Formula, T-Top<BR>1 of 20 Fieros in Switzerland||||reg||1
Z||000010||ray b||12-17-2001||12:44 PM||||well I like 914's but finding a unrusted out one is very hard<BR>sounds like the 924 maybe a parts car and that may be best way to make real $$$$ from this deal strip it many big parts are worth more than 200 each and you could get all your money back on your first sale!!!!<BR>924 are not a good home restore project and not worth cost of restore when done.<BR>make money and use it on the fiero.<P>------------------<BR>Question wonder and be wierd||||reg||1
Z||000011||TRiAD||12-17-2001||06:12 PM||||Or you could make it a real project and pu the 924 GT body on it with some killer engine...the GTs looked killer!<BR>||||reg||1
